A 28-year-old chef has been found dead in a San Francisco mall.
A 28-year-old chef who recently moved to San Francisco was found dead this week in the stairwell of a local mall.
However, the victim's brother said he couldn't think of anyone who wanted to hurt him, saying, "It's finally getting better."
San Francisco's coroner's office said the body found Wednesday morning at Xitan shopping mall was identified by 28-year-old San Francisco resident Frank Galicia.
The San Francisco Police Department said the case of the death had been ruled to kill him and that an investigation was underway.
The victim’s brother, Louis Galicia, told ABC’s San Francisco-based radio station KGO that Frank, who had previously worked as an assembly-line chef in Boston, had found an ideal job for an assembly-lined chef six months ago at San Francisco’s Sons & Daughters restaurant.
A spokesman for the Sons & Daughters restaurant said they were “very shocked” by Frank’s death.
“We are a small team that gets along like an intimate family, and we will miss him dearly,” the spokesperson said.
At this time of grief, we expressed our deep sympathies and condolences to Frank's family and friends.
Louis Galicia said Frank first lived in a guest house, but recently “everything is finally getting better.”
Louis Galicia told KGO: “Frank found an apartment with a girl at the same time.”
Louis Galicia said he couldn’t think of anyone who wanted to harm his brother.
Frank has a good heart and a broad heart.
According to Louis Galicia, “Frank often cooked dinner for his family.”
He never wanted to argue with his family.
He is the one who follows everyone’s opinions.
Louis Galicia said, "Everything in the world can go wrong, but Frank can be called a person who shines every day."
If you have a clue, please call the San Francisco Police Department to call 415-657-4444.
Junior doctors on strike: calls for new labor action
Representatives of junior doctors called on the Union to approve new labour actions in response to new contract disputes.
The British Medical Association’s Board of Primary Physicians will ask all of its members to support more labour initiatives starting in early September.
The Junior Doctors Committee said the ministers had not resolved their concerns about the contract.
Junior doctors and medical scientists voted in July to boycott a contract deal with the British Medical Association.
Fifty-eight percent of the members who participated in the vote objected to the contract transaction.
Ellen McCourt, chairman of junior doctors’ committee, said in a letter to members posted on Twitter late on Thursday that the government had “consistently remained silent” on the issues that led to a boycott of the contract.
“In view of this, the Executive Committee of the Junior Doctors Committee has voted to reject the proposed new contract in its entirety and has called for the resumption of formal consultations on all concerns,” she said.
In response to the government’s silent attitude, the Executive Committee of the Junior Doctors’ Committee has today formally requested a special meeting of the Council of British Medical Associations to approve a long-term plan aimed at escalating labour from the beginning of September.
The dispute has led junior doctors to take part in six strikes this year, including for the first time in the UK’s national health care system’s history.
Dr Johann Malawana, leader of junior doctors of the British Medical Association, resigned after a vote was boycotted by a vote on the contractual terms recommended by the British Medical Association.
In a pre-voting meeting of 54,000 junior doctors and medical students, he told members of the British Medical Association that the deal was good and should therefore be accepted.
After the vote, Health Minister Jeremy Hunt said the contract would be enforced among English medics.
Russia and Turkey: “Irrespective of the Union”?
A gesture to end a crisis.
The Russian and Turkish leaders met on Tuesday, shaking hands and announcing an official end to an eight-month-long war of words and economic sanctions.
However, when Vladimir Putin greeted the Turkish president in the gilded hall of a St. Petersburg palace, I had a strong feeling that this reconciliation was what Ankara would most like to see.
Yes, the two really shook hands.
However, even according to Mr. Putin’s always-reinforced standards, Putin is only a shallow smile, with almost no enthusiasm.
It is reported that Recep Tayyip Erdoğan mentioned "good friend" Putin five times.
I don't remember.
At the same time, he promised that relations with Russia would return to pre-crisis levels, or even higher levels.
The next day, a newspaper wrote that Mr. Erdoğan had behaved as if nothing had happened.
However, I think his enthusiasm hinted at something the opposite.
However, Mr. Putin’s continued indifference shows that he has forgotten nothing.
In fact, the first thing he mentioned in his opening remarks was the cause of the crisis: Turkey shot down a Russian warplane on the Syrian border.
Moscow’s response was intense.
Mr. Putin lashed out at the incident, accusing Ankara of thinking of Moscow.
Attacks from so-called friends are even more unacceptable.
It’s difficult, if not impossible, for both sides to re-establish true trust.
Since the beginning of November, Russia's public opinion has also been reversed.
For several months, government-controlled media carried out an all-out attack on Ankara.
Suddenly, the Turks seemed to be responsible for everything.
Among them, the most seriously came accusations from senior officials: Mr Erdogan’s family profited from illegal oil trading in the so-called ISIS-controlled region of Syria.
He categorically denied this claim.
However, news from St. Petersburg shows that it’s time to look ahead.
After all, this meeting presupposes that Putin has received an apology from Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan
Russia can claim victory.
For Ankara, the benefits of the truce are obvious.
First, Erdoğan narrowly ousted after last month’s failed coup, so he needs the support of all his friends.
It is clear that repeated terrorist attacks have taken a heavy toll on him.
At the same time, there are economic motives.
Russian sanctions have dealt a big blow to Turkey, in particular banning charter services that can transport millions of Russian tourists to Turkish shores each year.
This number has dropped by nearly 90%.
With the resumption of flights, Russia's travel agencies and charter companies will surely breathe a sigh of relief.
They will usher in the end-of-season Mediterranean tourism wave.
Even this week, state television predicted that the price of fruits and vegetables would fall once Turkey’s agricultural imports returned.
A report in the Moscow newspaper Vedemosti said: "Tourists are gone, the tomatoes are back."
For Moscow, the visit also has additional political value.
Ankara is outraged by the West’s feeble response to intent to take over.
In addition, Ankara is dissatisfied with the slow progress of EU accession talks and Putin's long-term interference, and Putin is keen to take advantage of political chills and weaken Turkey's relations with the Western world.
As a result of espousing elected authorities in the aftermath of the failed coup, Russian leaders are bound to receive additional points from Ankara.
Note that this is a gift to Moscow, which has always been deeply rooted in fear of regime change.
As a result, the meeting at this gilded seaside palace led Russia and Turkey to form an ally with two countries rejected by the Western world and abused, which one analyst described as “out of alliance.”
However, despite an open settlement, there are still major differences between the two sides.
Syria is one of the key factors. Moscow has recently played the role of peacemakers in Syria, while Russia and Turkey support the opposite faction.
It is foreseeable that after nearly three hours of preliminary talks, the two presidents said at the press conference that they have not yet talked about the topic.
Turkey’s president deliberately sidestepped questions about the differences between the two sides, while Putin emphasized it.
The two sides have not reached a clear consensus on how to find common ground on Syria.
The resumption of dialogue between the two leaders is certainly a good thing, thanks to months of open hostility and the possibility of a major disaster caused by the shooting down of Russian warplanes by Turkey, a member of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer serve customers outside of Scotland.
According to Royal Bank of Scotland’s chief executive, the bank will be reduced to a logistics unit.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will no longer serve clients outside of Scotland.
Ross McEwan told the BBC’s Scotland division that the Royal Bank of Scotland brand was once committed to its global ambitions.
But eight years ago on the verge of closure, had to accept the bailout since then has given up that kind of pursuit.
During that time, brand strategists used ‘RBS’ to protect other consumer finance brands.
The Group has spent millions of pounds to sponsor international sporting events, in the same way as rugby and Wimbledon tennis champion Andy Murray.
But now, the right thing to do is to make more national brands emerge.
The Royal Bank of Scotland will be used by Scottish customers, but will not revert to what is going on.
In England and Wales, all references outside headquarters and stock exchanges will be changed to National Westminster Bank.
The Ulster Bank brand has been used for customers in the Republic of Ireland and the Northern Territory.
There are also a number of other, more prominent smaller private banking brands, such as Capital Bank, Adam & Co, Drummond and Holt's Military Bank.
Mr McEone was interviewed during a tour of Inverness County clients and employees.
He told the BBC’s Scotland division: “The Royal Bank of Scotland brand will eventually become one of our investor brands, as well as one of the employee-employee brands, because in a larger sense, we have become a bank with numerous brands.”
Since the bank itself has become an international brand, RBS has also become a global brand.
For now, I’m saying that we’re no longer focused on the world, and we’ve turned our attention to home.
Each brand represents a difference in their respective industries, and our employees serve their customers through different brands.
The Royal Bank of Scotland has indicated that it will not continue to sponsor the six-country tournament; at the same time, efforts have been made to promote the image of different brands sponsoring sports events.
“It’s time to become a multi-brand bank, because privately we’ve been exploring how we’re going to be a bank that’s better serving our customers.”
If three years ago we decided to become a big bank with many brands, it would have been ridiculed.
At present, we focus on our customers' needs, and I am confident that you are aware of these changes.
“I think we can reboot these brands, and the time is right now.
Daturi achieves 3,000 career wins with Predilection in Newmarket
Newmarket's two wins have helped Ditturi achieve a career of 3,000 wins, after which he sprinkled champagne to celebrate.
On Friday, professional jockey Frankie Dettori scored his 3,000th victory in Newmarket.
Predilection by John Gosden, who was on the track, scored two victories that night, resulting in a career of 3,000 victories.
The 45-year-old Italian is the sixth professional jocker to achieve this milestone in the field of leveling.
The top five are: Sir Gordon Richards, Doug Smith, Lester Piggott, Pat Eddery and Willie Carson.
The three-time jockester who won earlier in Ghayyar said: "It's special."
I’m so excited because my family was there too.
“I’m happy with what I’ve achieved in Newmarket, because that’s where I started 30 years ago.
It’s not a large-scale Jockey Club, it’s not the Royal Ascot, it’s just an ordinary Friday night with 20,000 spectators.
I am happy and humble.
Daturi brought Predilection after winning, which caused cheers from the whole scene.
Two jockeys have achieved this in the field of steeplecks: champion jockey Richard Johnson and now-retired 20-year-old Sir Anthony McCoy, in which Sir McCoy achieves more than 4,300 victories in his record-breaking career.
Berger is a nine-time German champion with 4,493 wins in his career.
Mr. Daituli was one of the best jockeys of our time, so it’s no surprise that he’s achieved this milestone, and there aren’t many of them before him.
He’s stylish and healthy, but it’s what really makes him stand out in the weighing room is his tactical rationality.
He was an amazing equestrian ambassador, and over the years, his enthusiasm and charisma have attracted a lot of fans for the sport.
A few weeks ago, I was at Epsom and I told Dai Turi that if he wasn’t just a weekend with a big jockey, he would have been able to get 6,000 victories!
It’s so important to say true that Mr. Daturi is one of Britain’s best equestrian winners in three decades.
He had a lively personality, which was a good thing for British horse racing, but other than that, he was an incredible jockey.
Daturi celebrates this milestone with his signature jumping disempowerment.
From jumping dashes to jumping disembarks, Daturi has been a typical representative of the British field of leveling for the past two decades.
His career was like a roller coaster.
He won a number of championships, including the famous "seven consecutive wins" at the Royal Esco Jockey Club in 1996, but he also suffered a plane crash and was banned for dosing drugs.
Three years ago, when he returned to the field after the six-month ban, he was very difficult. However, he showed an amazing personality advantage and finally returned to the top, giving those skeptics the best.
Maybe he won’t be able to do that for too long – Dai Tulley has said before that he wants to compete at least until he’s 50.
Russian President Vladimir Putin removes Sergey Ivanov's Chief of Staff
Russian President Vladimir Putin unexpectedly removed Sergei Ivanov as chief of staff.
Over the years, Mr. Ivanov has been part of a core group that has earned Mr. Putin’s confidence.
Ivanov, 63, has now been appointed as special representative for environmental and transportation issues.
According to a statement from the Kremlin, Mr. Putin “declared the lifting of Ivanov’s post of chief of staff of the Russian president,” but no reason was given.
Anton Vaino, who began his role as Mr Ivanov’s deputy since 2012, has been appointed as his successor.
Mr. Vaino, 44, is a former diplomat.
He was born in 1972 in Tallinn, Estonia, and graduated from the prestigious Moscow State Institute of International Relations (MGIMO) and worked at the Embassy in Tokyo.
Later, he managed the presidential draft and government employees, as the Kremlin website (in Russian) said.
After his appointment, he said to Mr. Putin: “Thank you for your confidence.”
I believe that the most important task of this Government is to support the head of state in drafting laws and control directives.
Mr. Putin told Russian television on Friday that Mr. Ivanov himself had called for his resignation and had recommended Mr. Vaino to succeed himself.
Is such a smile convincing?
Moscow is puzzled by the move.
Sergey Ivanov has long been one of Putin’s closest aides and, like Putin, has also worked for the Federal Security Service (KGB) of the Soviet Union.
As chief of staff, he was one of the most influential people in the country.
At a meeting with President Vladimir Putin aired on state television, both said the chief of staff himself had called for their resignations.
Despite smiling in front of the camera, almost no one is convinced – especially at this time before the parliamentary elections.
So, is this due to the defeat of some kind of power competition?
It has not yet been known.
However, the official statement – a candidate who was once seen as a presidential candidate suddenly chooses to run Russia’s environmental policy – has raised many suspicions.
On the Kremlin’s website, quoting Mr. Ivanov’s dialogue with Mr. Putin, he said: “Yes, in early 2012, in a conversation, I asked you to give me this difficult position, or even a troubled position, for a four-year term.”
"As a result, I've been the President's Chief of Staff for four years and eight months."
Mr. Ivanov assumed this position in December 2011.
Before that, he served as deputy prime minister and defense minister.
He is a member of Russia’s Security Council and, like Mr. Putin, a former member of the National Security Agency.
In the late 1990s, when Mr. Putin became director of the Federal Security Service (FSB, formerly known as the National Security Service), Mr. Ivanov was appointed his deputy.
When Mr. Putin came to power, he appointed Mr. Ivanov one of his five most trusted men.
At one point, it had been believed that Mr. Ivanov was likely to become Russia’s president at the end of Mr. Putin’s second term, because three consecutive terms were unconstitutional.
However, this position ultimately belongs to Dmitry Medvedev, Putin’s other close aide.
Prior to three and a half years to return to office, Mr. Putin served as Prime Minister of Russia.
Mysterious Fish Surprises Banff's Garden
A resident of Banff found a fish in his garden and asked for help.
Kevin Bain believes that 75 fish may have been shaved by a tornado at sea.
Living about 500 meters from the sea, Mr. Bain posted a video of the strange scene on Periscope, hoping that someone could explain to him why these two-inch-long fish appeared.
The Queens Ferry Bridge is now accessible to Fave.
The £1.35 billion project is scheduled to be completed in May 2017.
Engineers have connected the northern bridge deck of the Queensferry Crossing and the viaduct, meaning that the bridge is now connected to Fave.
The four "bridge fans" Helong, located near the three bridgeheads of each bridge, are now close to completion, and the project is the first Helong bridge.
The bridge, which began in Faff, is 600 meters long and weighs 30,000 metric tons.
It uses 10,000 metric tons of steel, 20,000 metric tons of concrete, and 46 slanted lassos.
The £1.35 million project is scheduled to be completed in May 2017.
Economy Minister Keith Brown visited the scene today and became one of the first to climb the bridge.
“This is a historic moment in the construction of the Queens Feri Bridge,” he said.
We’ve all witnessed engineering work on such an ambitious project where more than 30,000 metric tons of concrete and steel were used in the construction of the bridge alone.
Although the size and weight of the bridge are very large, the completion of the joint between the viaduct and the bridge surface requires fine work and very accurate assembly.
Overall, nearly 79% of the total bridge surface is now installed, and the last section of the concrete bridge deck is poured in Rosyth today, which means that all the bridge decks are ready in place.
Michael Martin, project director of the Kunsferi bridge construction company, said: "No matter which bridge project, the first Helong is a major milestone."
As far as this remarkable project is concerned, Helong showcases cutting-edge civil engineering technologies.
After installing the last section of the road panel and connecting it to the enlarged North Bridge Fort Road panel, it is then necessary to complete the remaining notch joint on the north side or close to the land side.
Completing the task would require 700 mm to be moved south of the Northern Elevator Bridge.
It’s a huge job, and it’s also a very sophisticated operation.
It is said to be huge because the viaduct is 222 meters long, weighs about 6,000 metric tons, and needs to pull out about 3 per cent slope.
It’s precise because the engineering team needs to make sure that the deviation is very small, only a few millimeters on each side.
It’s gratifying that everything is going very well.
