Lessmann’s performance was a bit like revenge four years ago, when she was tied for third, but lost to Mustafaina in the tiebreaker.
But this time, the 22-year-old captain nicknamed "Grandma" by the Americans was far ahead.
She kissed the crowd at the end of free gymnastics and announced her return, but such a return sometimes seemed to be in doubt.
Byers is only 19 years old. Since earlier this week, the news has gone viral on Twitter. As far as Byers is concerned, his life has been given a new meaning.
Mahaj Brown, six-year-old, survived the Philadelphia shooting.
A six-year-old boy was shot in Philadelphia, the city’s second six-year-old shot this week, police said.
The child’s grandparents confirmed to CBS Philadelphia that Mahaj Brown was shot.
They said there were multiple gunshot wounds in Brown’s groin, legs, arms and abdomen, but the child was sober on the way to the hospital.
“He suffered a lot of sins, and his little body was broken by bullets,” his grandmother Delores Melendez told television.
I couldn't believe it.
The boy was in critical condition at the hospital and had undergone several surgeries.
His family told television that the child was expected to recover.
He was about to start first grade at a local elementary school.
Brown was one of four people shot dead in the Germantown area.
Of the three others who had been shot, two were 29 years old and one was 32 years old.
According to CBS Philadelphia, one of the 29-year-old victims was in critical condition; the other two were in stable condition.
TV said the child and the 29-year-old victim had been found in the front seat of the car.
The other two victims were not in the car, and police are investigating whether among the injured were gunmen.
Investigators told CBS Philadelphia that it looked as if multiple gunmen had exchanged fire.
A large number of assault weapons were found at the scene, and it can be inferred that they were using different assault weapons.
In other words, this is an ongoing battle.
There are bullet casings on the sidewalks on both sides of the street.
“So, there were several armed men at the scene who were in a state of intense fighting,” Philadelphia Police Chief Anthony Ginaldi told television.
Many houses and vehicles were damaged in the area.
Arrests have not yet been carried out, and it is said that the police are watching surveillance videos.
On Tuesday, a six-year-old (girl) was also ejected into her arm while playing on the doorstep of the West Oak Lane neighborhood.
She is currently recovering, and the police are still pursuing the suspects.
Why do we need Simon, Gabrielle and Laurie?
That’s how I rated this year’s women’s Olympic gymnastics team for more than one reason.
First of all, since 1984, I have been to every Summer Olympics, but the United States has never had such hegemony.
Secondly, this gymnastics team has three excellent gymnasts—Simone Biles, Gabby Douglas and Lauren Laurie Hernandez—who have always inspired girls of color.
The composition of this team is very proud, because it includes Aly Raisman of Judaism and Madison Kocian of the Catholic Church.
This team finally looks like the U.S. team.
It also solidifies black and Latino women’s pinnacle in the sport.
Over the past four years, leading female gymnasts have been African-Americans.
Last year, Simon and Gabrielle were ranked first and second.
That's great.
At the last Olympic Games, Gabriel Douglas "unexpectedly" became the Olympic champion, which made the United States happy.
Today, the whole world is able to witness three-time world champions Simon Byers, Douglas and Laurie Hernandez's outstanding performances. They have deduced conventional gymnastics techniques.
On Thursday, Simon helped the U.S. team achieve four consecutive titles in the women’s individual all-around program.
Her team-mate, Ali Lesman, won the silver medal.
We’ve reached the top of the sport, but that wasn’t the case in gymnastics classrooms across the country.
Simon, Gabrielle and Laurie have the same access to the Olympic team as Ali and Madison did.
They’re “Final Five” because the next Olympic gymnastics team will be made up of four gymnasts, Marta Karolyi’s final year.
However, the reality is that gymnastics is an expensive sport, gymnasts are mainly trained in private gymnastics clubs, and their parents need to pay for training, facilities, travel, clothing, competitions and hotels (including coaches and gymnasts and their families), and the cost list is constantly increasing.
Most gymnastics clubs are located in affluent suburbs that can support the industry.
All the training of gymnasts is done in these clubs, which is a long-term and expensive expense for the gymnast's family.
Byers coach Aimee Boorman and Hernandez’s coach, Maggie Haney, have coached them ever since the two gymnasts began practicing gymnastics.
We must bring more affordable and accessible gymnastics training to countless young girls influenced by Simon, Gabriel and Laurie.
Twenty years ago, I set up my own gymnastics foundation.
In my organization, we offer free, cheap gymnastics to more than 15,000 urban youth (mainly black and Spanish-Americans) in black residential neighborhoods, and produce national and even world championships.
In September, we’ll be expanding into Detroit (my hometown), offering high-quality gymnastics to young people who deserve this learning opportunity and who will benefit from this amazing Olympic sport.”
Gymnastics helps to cultivate the strength, flexibility and coordination of the body, as well as the development of hard-working spirit, self-discipline and determination.
These can bring lifelong benefits to a person’s health, schooling, and career success.
Encouraged by this team, it’s really great that thousands of young girls are going to study gymnastics.
Participating in sports is important, especially for girls.
This is an exciting time.
Thank you to Simon, Ali, Laurie, Gabriel and Madison for their “girl power” in the process of winning Olympic team gold medals, as well as for inspiring so many young girls from different backgrounds to embark on the road to health, strength and success.
After dismissing Jeremy Hunt’s proposal, junior doctors plan to launch further strike
Ministers initially hoped to end the change-related dispute by accepting a modified contract from British Medical Association (BMA) officials, but 58 per cent of the 37,000 junior doctors who participated in the vote opposed the contract.
In July, Mr Hunt revealed plans to enforce the contract, saying the NHS was currently in a “vacuum zone” and that a slight delay would harm the interests of employees and patients.
The new contract begins by referring to changes to the practices of junior doctors working all night and weekends in order to improve care during non-regular working hours.
Last night, Dr. Ellen McCourt, chairman of the BMA’s Junior Doctors Committee, said in a statement: “Junior doctors still have serious concerns about the proposed contract, especially as it exacerbates the current labor crisis and fails to treat all doctors fairly.”
It’s only been eight weeks since the first doctors signed the new contract, and we need to make progress and there’s not much time left.
The BMA tried to resolve the dispute through negotiations, but everyone was reluctant to participate, and sometimes the government responded in silence.
Nevertheless, just last month we got Jeremy Hunt’s promise that his doors were always open.
“Jeremy Hunt now needs to take action, eliminate enforcement, and address the concerns of junior doctors.”
If he doesn’t do that, junior doctors have made it clear that they’re ready to take further labor action.
The last strike, which took place at the end of April, resulted in nearly 13,000 routine surgeries and 100,000 appointments being postponed as nurses and consultation doctors covered the picket line.
Ministers initially hoped to end the change-induced dispute by accepting the BMA’s revised contract.
The BMA wants to fight for better weekend pay for the 54,000 junior doctors covered by the contract.
Daniel Mortimer, CEO of the NHS Employers, called on junior doctors not to stage any more strikes.
Mr Mortimer said: “Labour operations have had very little success, but on the contrary, they will put pressure on teams and services that are already dedicated, resulting in worries, worries and confusion among patients, caregivers and their families.”
Over the past two months, we have been in constant negotiations with the Committee of Primary Physicians and, together with the Ministry of Health and others, have responded positively to their concerns about the role of guardians and whistleblowers.
Employers want to continue to actively explore other important topics such as deployment, the flexibility of training, other training for those who have re-emerged after a career break, the cost of training, a common understanding of the curriculum, further study leave, and gender wage differentials in the medical field, illustrating the importance that employers, Health Education England, and the Department of Health have agreed with BMA in November, February and May.
A £2-a-day drug reduces the threat of a heart attack.
Officials have suggested that a £2-a-day drug should be rolled out to more patients and that it would take thousands of people with heart disease to benefit from it.
Anticoagulants (ticagrelor) may reduce the risk of recurrence of heart disease in patients with heart disease.
The drug, which lasts for 12 months after a heart attack, may reduce the risk of another stroke or heart attack.
The National Institute for Health and Clinical Optimization (NICE), the NHS’s regulator, recommends that patients continue to take medication for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.
But now the NHS regulator, NICE, has recommended that patients be given medication continuously for four years in order to further reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.
In England, about 140,000 people develop heart disease each year, and one in four of them will continue to suffer a recurrence of heart disease or a stroke.
Heart attacks and strokes are caused by the formation of plaque in the deposition of fatty substances in arterial walls.
If the plaque is disconnected, it will lead to blood clots, which in turn hinders the flow of blood into the heart, resulting in a heart attack.
If the thrombosis moves, it enters the blood flow, hindering the flow of blood into the brain, resulting in a stroke.
In patients who have experienced a heart attack, there is a higher risk of another heart attack.
Manufactured by British company AstraZeneca and marketed under the brand name Brilique, ticagre can reduce the risk of onset by reducing the formation of blood clots.
Today’s NICE draft recommended that patients take 90 mg of ticagre for 12 months, followed by 60 mg per day and aspirin twice daily for the next three years.
Professor Carole Longson, director of NICE’s Centre for Health Technology Assessment, said: “Despite effective secondary prevention treatments, as many as one in four patients who have had a history of having a heart attack can get sick again or have another stroke, often with serious consequences.”
The fear of recurrence can have a large negative impact on a person's quality of life.
The evidence shows that ticagre taking it with aspirin can effectively reduce the risk of heart disease recurrence and stroke in those who have experienced a heart attack.
We are pleased that the temporary recommendation of Ticagre will provide more treatment options for thousands of people who have benefited.
Due to the limited information about the efficacy and safety of ticagre after three years, in particular the risk of bleeding, the draft guidance does not recommend that it continue to be treated after this period.
Computer culture makes ‘electival fraud’ prevalent in Muslim areas
Lutfur Rahman, the former mayor of Tower Hamlets, was sacked last year for corruption, prompting the publication of a government report.
“Political correctness’ culture has blinded Muslim communities to widespread electoral fraud, as this lethal government report reveals today.”
Sir Eric Pickles, a former cabinet minister, accused the police, election regulators and City Hall of turning a blind eye to evidence of abuse of elections because they were “excessively sensitive to ethnic divisions and religious issues.”
The former minister of community affairs and current anti-corruption chief said that the practice of intimidating voters with a religious position should have a bad impact, and the police should be allowed to set up a cordon outside the polling station to protect frightened voters.
He advised voters to carry identity documents to confirm their identities when they cast their ballots.
At present, voters only need to report their names and addresses.
This report even recommends applying ink that cannot be erased on the fingers of voters to prevent people from voting twice.
Sir Eric said the protection of whistleblowers should be increased.
He called on the media to increase their awareness of parliamentary documentation and the means by which decisions taken by non-official committees or working groups were made.
“It’s not just about guarding against electoral fraud, it’s about protecting local governments from a widespread culture of corruption and financial fraud that’s closely linked to them,” he said.
The report was requested by Downing Street in the wake of the Tower Hamlets election scandal in East London.
Last year, a special electoral tribunal ruled in the existence of a series of acts of corruption and illegality that should be removed from his position as mayor of Rahman.
The court found that Rahman was "very ludicrously abused" by the religious sentiments of the Muslim community and kept silent about the sword's allegations against him of racial discrimination and Islamophobia.
It is said that Rachman “dedicated during the campaign that it was a religious duty to give him a vote.”
As many as 300 ballots are in doubt, and it may be that voters have faked someone else’s vote.
Sir Eric said there was evidence of electoral fraud across the country.
In particular, he warned of mail-in electoral fraud, and senior members of the Muslim community are said to have put pressure on voters to vote for their designated candidates.
Sir Erick (pictured) made a number of suggestions for clearing the voting mechanism, which included bringing a good ID to confirm his identity.
He said in his report: “Evidence shows that senior members, especially those in Pakistani and Bangladeshian backgrounds, are putting pressure on vulnerable groups in some ethnic minority communities, especially women and young people, to vote according to their will.”
“Because of their oversensitivity to racial divisions and religious ‘political correctness’, they fear that state institutions will turn a blind eye to such behavior.”
Sir Erick made 50 proposals to clean up the electoral mechanism, including banning political activists from handling postal voting documents to stop the "voting harvest".
The report also calls for a rigorous check of the registration information to prevent voter rolls from being used for immigration and welfare scams. Theresa May is also considering the report.
These recommendations include requiring voters to present identity documents before voting.
After the vote, it is painted on the fingers of the voters that can't be erased, so as to prevent someone from voting twice.
Systematically verifies the ethnic affiliation of voters in order to ensure eligibility to vote.
The validity period of the automatic postal voting request shall be set at three years.
Political activists and radicals are prohibited from dealing with completed postal and postal voting envelopes.
Police are authorized to set up cordons around polling stations.
Amendments to the provisions of the law have made it easier to bring intimidation charges under the Representation of the People’s Act 1983.
It is illegal to take pictures at polling stations.
It is required to use English only at polling stations (except in Wales).
Training has been strengthened to ensure that staff strictly enforce the requirement of “voters going to the voting room alone”.
Sir Erick lambasted the Metropolitan Police, saying they had made no criminal charges in the wake of the Tower Hamlets incident, which was truly “surprising”.
“Rachman and his election officers had a lot of corruption and illegal behavior, so they were disqualified by the electoral court,” he said.
According to the standards of criminal proof, he is in no doubt guilty in many ways.
He also criticized the virtual "check box inspection" of the election registration department of the city hall.
Sir Eric said: “Last year’s court decision on the Tower Hamlets was a wake-up call for everyone.”
China has a glorious tradition of "mother of parliament", but it is a real concern that some institutions have secretly sneaked up on electoral fraud and, in the aftermath, have also threatened this good reputation.
