CBC Olympic narrator apologizes for comments that Chinese swimmers are 'as slow as pigs'
On Wednesday, Byron MacDonald irritated viewers watching the Canadian Broadcasting Corporation (CBC) Olympic live broadcast.
CBC’s CBC’s narrator said 14-year-old Chinese swimmers “swim faster than stinking” at the competition, and ended up “swipe like a pig,” after which CBC was forced to apologize.
Narrator Byron Macdonald gave some of his opinion at the end of the women’s 4x200 m freestyle relay final, when he thought his microphone had been turned off.
Viewed Byron, a commenter from the CBC narrator’s team who wore “colored glasses,” said: “The 14-year-old Chinese girl made a stupid mistake.”
Because it was too excited, it began to swim faster than the stink, but then as slowly as a pig.
What a loss to her.
The 14-year-old Chinese swimmer, Ai, took 1:57.79 — 1.61 seconds behind Canada’s Taylor Ruck, which in turn narrowed the gap with China.
The entire Canadian audience was struck by the narrator’s tone and phrasing, after which they quickly reacted online.
Emmett Macfarlane tweeted: “The CBC commentator said that China’s 14-year-old swimmer was ‘as slow as a pig’?”
This idiot didn’t realize that it was broadcast live.
The Chinese junior general who participated in the women's 4x200-meter freestyle relay final was described in this way: "The 14-year-old Chinese girl made a stupid mistake."
Criticism of Byron Macdonald on Twitter quickly came on Twitter, and many people asked him to withdraw from the commentary.
When Twitter users heard that Byron MacDonald was unresponsive, they didn’t stop there.
Sarah Paradis, another Twitter user, wrote: “Wow #Byron MacDonald.
You're representing CBC and Canada, and you're making us all sound like a bunch of racists. #It's disgusting.
Soon after, CBC broadcaster Scott Russel apologised for Byron's comments, saying: "We apologize for our comments about the performance of live swimmers."
“We are sorry for the emergence of such untimely wording.”
CBC apologizes more than 90 times to Twitter users who responded to the comments
Scott Russel, another narrator, also apologized on behalf of Byron.
CBC then copied and pasted their apology to more than 90 other Twitter users protesting Byron’s explanation.
The channel also quickly apologized and issued a statement.
We are very sorry to say something like this and broadcast it live.
Last night, we acted quickly to apologize to the viewers who watched our live broadcasts, as well as the viewers who followed us on social media.
It should be noted that Byron's explanation is aimed at the swimmer's performance, but it is not for her personally.
That is to say, the wording is really inappropriate, and Byron is deeply sorry about it.
On Thursday afternoon, Macdonald apologized in a live broadcast, saying that his words did not mean a personal attack, and tried to justify his remarks.
“I wanted to take the time to apologize for what I said last night after the women’s relay,” he said on the CBC Olympics.
"I'm just commenting on the swimmer's performance, not the individual."
"Of course, I don't mean disrespect, I'm sorry."
Primark customers are outraged by a woman who brazenly tries her underwear in the middle of the store.
A woman undressed her underwear on the spot in the center of a Primark store, and the in-store customers didn't know where to look.
To make matters worse, she looked at what it looked like when she put it on, and then took off her underwear and put it back on the shelves.
She continued to browse through the shelves, and eventually tried four panties inside this shop at the Bouverie Place shopping center in Folkestone.
The woman kept trying on her panties at the Primark shop in Folkestone's Bouverie Place shopping centre before putting them back on the shelves.
Jenny Davidson, who was also inside the store at the time (the mother of two), said that after trying on her underwear, the mysterious woman "dressed again and walked out of the store and didn't buy anything."
She said, "Everyone can't believe what they've seen."
She stood in the middle of the store, took down a pair of panties from the shelves, took off his leggings and underwear, and then put it on like this.
"She didn't try to find a hidden place to try it on - she just stood there, it's brazen."
"When she took her underwear off again, everyone was stunned. Obviously, she was still not satisfied, because she put the underwear back on the shelf."
After that, she tried three times like this again, and finally found that it was not what she liked, and then got dressed again and walked out of the store.
“She didn’t seem to care if someone was looking at her — or who would end up buying the panties she was trying to wear.”
Ms Davidson (33), who lives in Kent, said: "It's disgusting to think that someone else is going to buy the underwear that other people wear and they haven't been cleaned."
This happened on Tuesday afternoon.
Ms Davidson added: “After she left, it was clear that the clerks were going to take off these panties, fortunately.”
“I hated buying a new pair of underwear home, only to find that there was another woman who tried it and wasn’t cleaned up.”
A spokesman for Primark said: “Primark is aware of what happened at our Folkestone store on Tuesday, August 9th.”
All problematic goods have been removed.
How to protect yourself by using maga in a gun attack
It’s very dangerous, but this video on Dailymail.com may give you a sense of security.
Let’s ask Rhon Mizrachi of New York’s Krav Maga Federation to learn how to deal closely with the perpetrators.
Whether it’s coming from the front or the back, the video will teach you how to win.
Mizrachi said that if the gunman approaches you from behind, the first thing you have to do is turn around so that you can see the attacker and “clear the line of fire.”
Next, clamp the gun with your forearm and shoulders, and hit the attacker in the face with your elbow.
Next with his knees in his groin, when the attacker bended in pain, turned the gun in his hand and punched him in the face with the barrel and back-of-the-gun, keeping the weapon all the way to him.
If the gunman is approaching from the front, a similar technique is used – to keep your body out of the line of fire, to seize the gun and twist the muzzle, so that the gunman can’t open fire.
At the same time, put the weight on the gun and hit the attacker’s chest, and turned the muzzle from his clenched hand before pointing the muzzle at him.
Does that sound complicated?
Check out the video to see exactly what Mizrachi is doing.
But remember, if you’re not a magga master, it’s the safest way to get rid of robberies by handing over cash.
A rogue family destroys low-cost rentals in Soltash, Cornwall, before being deported.
A rogue family who had been evicted for intimidating their neighbours caused £50,000 in damage to the low-rent housing they lived in before they left.
After a long list of complaints, Tanya Skeldon and her husband Shaun Trebilcock were evicted from a low-cost rental house in Saltash, Cornwall.
After being forcibly removed from the residence, Housing Association officials were surprised to find that it had been severely damaged.
Holes were everywhere on the walls, the floors were rippled, the garbage was littered, and the walls were scribbled.
After being evicted, the family has been moving and is said to be currently living in a private home.
Donna McEvoy, home manager at the Cornwall Housing Association, said she was worried the house was unsalvaged.
“It’s a nightmare for the people who live here, and I don’t think people can live like this,” she said.
There’s a lot to weigh on right now, because this home is likely to be a not-so-good element in Cornwall.
The family is notorious, and their names are equally infamous.
The couple, who have three sons, live here from time to time. Police say the family's acts of terror include the use of baseball bats for fighting in the streets, intimidating nearby residents, using drugs, drug trafficking, shouting, arguing and swearing.
In June, the 47-year-old tenant named Skeldon received an anti-social behavior resolution.
In this house, which may have been demolished, many rooms are littered for a long time.
In this once-trading drug room, the floor was lifted up.
Neighbors said they often saw them fighting in the streets and often called the police as a result.
After being given an 11-week extension to allow them to voluntarily move away, the Cornwall Housing Association forcibly removed her and her husband this week.
Neighbors who were finally relieved cheered by it, and they said that their previous lives were hell on earth.
A neighbor said, "They don't care about anybody."
We’ve lived next door to them for 20 years and it’s always been so bad.
We’ve seen the police many times.
“We couldn’t sleep this morning.”
We got up early, because it was too quiet – we weren’t used to it yet!
It’s great that they’re finally gone.
Adam Fitzpatrick, community executive manager of the Cornwall Housing Association, said the family did not cooperate well with the local government.
The family lived in the house for 20 years, and the police often visited.
Garbage and old toys were thrown on the weedy side of the low-rent housing.
He added: “Regrettably, the tenant has not taken any responsibility for the conduct of her and her family, and the eviction was the last consideration by the Cornwall Housing Association.”
It’s a very difficult case because they’ve behaved very extremely, and what they’re doing must have terrified neighbors.
I would like to thank my team for the work they have done, as well as Devon and Cornwall Police, as well as members of the courageous community who provided us with information.
I hope that this will bring some comfort to our neighbors and send a message that we do not tolerate anti-social behavior.
Mr Fitzpatrick said his three sons had lived in the house at various times, but said they were all in their 20s.
After they were driven away, one of the bedrooms also threw a dirty mattress on the floor.
Housing association workers will soon clean up piles of garbage around the house
In 2014, police raided the house and found more than 70 grams of marijuana, as well as balance, drug utensils and £1,700 in cash.
Skeldon was fined for possession of Class B drugs and drug trafficking.
She claims that the money was used to buy tombstones for Lee Skeldon, the son who died in a car accident in 2013.
It is understood Tanya and her husband are now living in a private house in Plymouth after becoming homeless.
The walls of many rooms of the house are covered with graffiti.
Police said they would evict tenants who defied the tenant’s rules.
Angela Crow of the Devon and Cornwall Police Department said the eviction issued a clear warning to other tenants who continue to defy tenant rules.
“This warning is aimed at people who believe that their actions will not be blamed, and those who are devastated by it,” she said.
Devonshire and Cornwish Police will not tolerate crime and anti-social behaviour, and they will continue to work with partners to protect our communities.
