VIDEO: Small-headed sleeping shark is the longest-lived vertebrate
According to scientists, Greenland sharks are the longest-lived vertebrates on Earth.
Julius Nielsen, who has been studying sharks, said that according to the data, there is a female small-headed sleeping shark that is estimated to be between 272 and 512 years old, and the body is up to five meters long.
French court rejects petition to demolish shops in jungle camps
A French court has rejected a petition by the Calais government to demolish dozens of makeshift shops and restaurants in the "Jungle" camp, which is currently inhabited by thousands of refugees.
At the court's hearing this week, Calais argued that the French government should have the right to remove a total of 72 makeshift shops in the camp. People in these shops in the camps sell goods ranging from cigarettes to energy drinks, provide barber services, and sell tea for 50 cents (40 pence) or basic meals for a few euros in temporary restaurants.
The office of the Governor of Calais believes that these places should be demolished because they are a parallel economy that does not pay taxes, and there are also fire and health hazards.
Humanitarian groups have indicated to the courts that these shops and restaurants are extremely important, as free food provided by state-supported associations and other groups simply does not meet the growing needs of refugees in the camps.
They claim that these makeshift shops and restaurants often provide shelter and free food for people in need.
A court in Lille ruled that there was no legal basis to deport the operators of the 72 temporary shops, but said it was “entirely understandable” the concerns of the Calais governor.
Judge Jean-François Molla said that these grocery stores, cafes and restaurants serve much more than simply providing food to refugees “living in extremely dangerous conditions.”
He said these places are a calm meeting point between refugees and volunteers.
One of the places threatened with demolition is the “Jungle Book Kids” cafe in the camp, a non-profit shop that provides 200 food, English and French lessons per day, as well as asylum advice for hundreds of unaccompanied and vulnerable minors in the camp.
More than 170,000 people signed a petition to protect the cafe.
In a letter to the Guardian, it was written that closing the café “would be a disaster for these children, many of whom are only eight years old.”
Two philanthropic organizations, Help Refugees and L’Auberge des Migrants, estimated on Friday that the number of refugees in jungle camps had topped 9,000, the highest level since the surge in refugee numbers in the summer.
“That $5,000 has solved my urgency.”
