Third-party companies (non-Chinese companies) commissioned by the Olympic Organizing Committee to make the national flag are located in Sao Paulo, Brazil, about 75 kilometers from the city of São Paulo.
In order to urge the manufacturers to complete as soon as possible, the Chinese Consulate General in Sao Paulo organized a special person to go to the manufacturers and do in-depth work.
After 30 hours of working overtime, the manufacturer finally completed the production of the Chinese flag and loaded the car to transport, and completed the task that originally took 4-5 working days in advance.
It is reported that the national flag is expected to arrive in Rio on the 11th, and the Olympic Organizing Committee will receive the flag and send it to various Olympic venues, and the whole process will be completed under the supervision of the Chinese embassies and consulates in Brazil.
On the 10th, Nantong municipal government held the Shanghai International Convention Center, Shanghai industry cooperation talk activities, more than 180 Shanghai enterprises were invited to participate, more than 20 million yuan Shanghai industry docking project on-site signing.
The total investment of the 20 projects signed is 8.063 billion yuan, involving many industries such as new energy, new materials, and modern service industries.
Qingdao is the 12th city in the country and the first pilot city in Shandong Province for paid use and trading.
On the 11th, a number of enterprises came to the Qingdao sewage rights trading center to submit the right to requisition, which will pay paid royalties after acceptance.
The upcoming first transaction also indicates that the pilot work of Qingdao's sewage rights trading has officially kicked off, which will also be the first list of sewage rights transactions in the province and the first transaction of municipal-level sewage rights in the country.
On the morning of the 11th, Zhao Xiuping, general manager of Qingdao Shuangtao Fine Chemical (Group) Co., Ltd., came to the Qingdao sewage rights trading center to submit sewage rights for purchase.
At the end of last year, the company's factory in Qingdao's old town was completely discontinued, and environmental protection was relocated to Pingdu's new factory.
After Zhao Xiuping submitted the application form, the environmental impact report of the construction project and other materials, the Qingdao sewage rights reserve and trading service center began to carry out the preliminary examination of the application materials.
According to the collection standards determined by prices, finance and other departments of Qingdao City, the current pre-estimated initial sewage rights royalty is about 641,200 yuan, and the staff will complete the approval within 7 working days.
The imminent completion of the first transaction also indicates that the pilot work of Qingdao sewage rights trading has officially kicked off, and it will also be the province's first list of sewage rights transactions and the first national city-level sewage rights trading.
Zhao Xiuping said that at present, the company's environmental protection has invested 400 million yuan, and the amount is huge on the surface, but it has a great impact on the enterprise's management philosophy and social benefits, and has also opened up space for enterprise innovation and management.
On the same day, Qingdao Sifang Xincheng Zhizhuo Bus Parts Co., Ltd. also applied for sewage rights for the annual output of 4,200 pieces of high-speed rail and EMU interior accessories projects.
The project belongs to the machining enterprise, and the initial sewage right applied for is 0.514 tons/year of chemical oxygen demand (COD) and 0.052 tons/year of ammonia nitrogen (NOX).
The paid use and trading of air pollutant discharge rights is for key industrial enterprises in the city's power and heat production and supply.
The existing sewage units that have obtained the EIA before January 1, 2016 will not levy paid royalties.
“Reimbursable access to sewage rights is not the same as obtaining the right to illegal discharge, and it is impossible to discharge pollutants at will.”
Han Hongyan, deputy director of the Qingdao Municipal Environmental Protection Bureau, said that if illegal discharge and excessive sewage still need to be punished in accordance with relevant laws and regulations, it is impossible to allow illegal enterprises to avoid punishment by purchasing sewage rights.
In other words, through legal means to crack down on environmental violations and severely punish enterprises for excessive polluting behavior, so as to protect the environment and protect public health.
At present, for their own interests, enterprises have increased their enthusiasm for pollution control, cherished limited sewage rights, and reduced pollutant emissions.
Driven by economic interests, reducing pollutant emissions has changed from the coercive behavior of the government to the conscious market behavior of enterprises, so as to truly achieve the purpose of protecting the environment, protecting nature, and protecting public health.
Pollution rights, also known as "emission rights", are the right to discharge pollutants, which refers to the right of emitters to discharge pollutants into the environment in accordance with the law within the amount allocated by the government.
This right is essentially the right of the defecators to occupy, use and benefit from environmental capacity resources.
In the next step, under the premise that the total amount of pollutant emissions does not exceed the allowable emissions, the internal polluting sources can adjust each other's emissions through currency exchange.
Since 1968, when the U.S. economist Dales proposed it, the U.S. began applying it to the management of atmospheric and river sources of pollution in the 1970s.
China has been piloting since 2007, and 14 provinces (autonomous regions and municipalities) such as Zhejiang and Jiangsu have been fully implemented.
According to the Russian Ministry of Defense, in order to assist these six strategic bombers to carry out air strikes against "Islamic State" targets in Syria, a number of Russian military aircraft were lifted off from Hmeimim Air Base in Syria.
On February 22 this year, the United States and Russia reached an agreement on a ceasefire between all parties to the conflict in Syria, and the ceasefire agreement began to be implemented on the 27th of the same month.
According to the agreement, the ceasefire does not include military strikes against terrorist organizations designated by the United Nations Security Council, such as the Islamic State Support Front.
Russia will continue to build relevant military infrastructure at the base, and the living conditions of Russian soldiers will also be guaranteed accordingly.
