Some media said that after the British referendum to leave the EU, Britain's new prime minister chose to go to the "neutral country" on the European continent for a vacation in Switzerland, with some kind of policy symbolism.
The British "Guardian" commented that the symbolic role represented by Switzerland, a neutral country, played a role in the choice of the British Prime Minister's first resort.
Britain's "Daily Telegraph" commented that Theresa May chose to go on holiday in Switzerland, which is the first time after Margaret Thatcher, the British Prime Minister went to Switzerland for a vacation.
The choice of the two female prime ministers contrasted with Blair and Cameron's preference for a vacation in hotter places.
Britain’s new prime minister, Theresa May, will return to London on August 24, after waiting for her is a series of important jobs and activities.
The British Parliament will resume on September 5, and the topics of the debate will include an online request for a second Brexit referendum.
On June 23, the United Kingdom voted to leave the EU, but Britain's new prime minister is not willing to launch the 50th paragraph of the Lisbon Treaty on member countries' Brexit by the end of the year.
