In recent days, 76ers coach Brett-Blunn gave Sarwich a high rating, and the Spurs manager believes he saw the shadow of Manu Ginobili when he was young.
It’s clear that Sarric has shown his talent and has kept the 76ers looking forward to him.
In Croatia’s first two games at the Olympics, Saric scored 24 points, 17 rebounds and 12 assists.
In the first half, Sarric showed his ability to pull the space on the field, and by blocking off his teammates, he was able to shoot outside the three-point line.
Although the 76ers currently hoard a lot of talented young players, they don’t have Sarich’s external ability.
From the hood to launching a long-pass fast attack and completing assists, there are only a handful of insider players who can do this in today's NBA games.
Since the start of the Olympic Games, Saric has averaged 13 points, eight rebounds and five assists in three games.
Since deciding to return to the Cavaliers in the summer of 2014, James has signed two 1+1 contracts in a row.
As the NBA’s current collective bargaining agreement enters its final year, as vice-chairman of the NBA players’ union, James will be at the forefront of collective bargaining negotiations.
In view of the sharp increase in the league's revenue, thanks to the new telecast agreement, as well as the battle between bosses and players on how to deal with the money, there are concerns that the NBA will be shut down next summer.
But for James, he doesn’t have to worry about becoming a free agent next summer because he’s renewed a three-year contract with the Cavaliers.
