The future of the Portland smelter is fraught with uncertainty due to the cancellation of the power supply contract.
As the power company, which supplies electricity to the plant, revealed that a plant owner will terminate a supply contract, invalidating all supply contracts, hundreds of workers at the Portland aluminum smelter in Victoria are currently facing an uncertain future.
The move comes after the Victorian government decided not to continue issuing subsidies to guarantee plant operations.
The 20-year fixed subsidy will end in November.
2,000 workers at the Portland plant could face job losses.
Once the factory closes, up to 2,000 people may lose their jobs directly or indirectly.
And because the plant consumes about 10% of Victoria’s total electricity output, there may also be a chain reaction.
In 2014, Aluminium shut down the Hanry Point smelter, which led to last year’s closure of the Anglesey power plant.
Aluminium's Portland smelter is currently losing money and is at risk of closure.
The move came after a forecast of the factory in the next few years, initially due to the strength of the Australian dollar, and more recently because of the price of the factory’s products.
AGL supplied electricity for the large aluminum smelter through the La Troeb Valley Old Åøjan power plant. The company predicts that three other plant owners will also terminate their agreements.
The largest shareholder of the plant is a joint venture between Aluminium and Alumina Company, while CITIC Group in China and Marubeni Co., Ltd. in Japan also hold a small stake.
“AGL anticipates that the Portland plant will continue to operate, which means that this smelter will continue to generate electricity demand.”
AGL stated that these contracts were “hedging contracts” that were supposed to take effect from November 2016.
The termination period of the contract is 12 months, so they will be terminated in 2017.
“As a result, AGL still predicts that these contracts will continue to generate revenue in fiscal year 2017.”
Any revenue effects that may occur on AGL since fiscal 2018 are currently subject to an optimistic outlook for wholesale electricity prices in futures markets.
AGL said the company had written down $187 million in contract value in the “Expedients for Termination Activities.”
Aluminium said its decision to terminate those contracts “will not affect Aluminium’s ability to operate the smelter or guarantee the smelter’s power supply.”
The U.S. company said it signed a 20-year-old contract with Ey Young in 2010 that was supposed to take effect in November, but the company did not explain why it pulled out of the contract.
Analysts are cautious about whether the Portland smelter in Victoria, as well as the Tomargo plant near Newcastle, will be shut down due to the industry's poor economic situation.
In response to comments made by analysts earlier this week, AGL’s head of operations Steven Mickelson said that Portland’s annual electricity consumption reaches about 600 megawatts and the Tomargo plant reaches about 900 megawatts.
If the two factories close, the demand in the electricity market will be greatly reduced.
He said: "As a power company, this is not good news for us."
Community law centers keep 160,000 people out every year.
Every year, more than 160,000 of the most vulnerable are turned away by community legal centers. Funding for this service is already overstretched, and further funding cuts are now being faced.
In Queensland, three out of every five people are denied access to free legal advice and agency services, mostly seniors, migrants, vulnerable workers, victims of violence and those who do not have the resources to fight unfair evictions.
The lack of funds for community legal services has long exceeded the limit and is now facing further funding cuts.
Starting next June, federal funding for these legal centers will be reduced by another 30 percent.
James Farrell, head of the Queensland Community Law Centre, said thousands of people were turned away every year, according to the latest National Community Association Law Centre survey.
Mr Farrell said the situation would only get worse if the federal government did not change existing decisions.
He said: “Queensland’s community law centres have long been underfunded, which means that thousands of people will not be able to access the vital services they need.”
People who need to stay away from violence, people with huge debts that can’t be repaid, families who are facing disintegration, workers who are being squeezed are facing legal problems, and the report shows that they don’t have access to the help they need.
The federal government will cut funding by an additional 30 per cent from June 2017, which means that thousands of vulnerable people in Queensland will be forced to face complex legal issues without help.
We call on George Brandeis, Minister of Justice and Member of Parliament in Queensland, to immediately rescind these funding cuts so that people can get the help they need in the face of legal issues.
Last year, the state government promised to raise legal aid funding to the national average.
Attention must now be paid to community legal centres and their needs for corresponding communities, and the Baileki government must also be involved in this important work.
Donald Trump campaign indicted for staff pulling guns
NEW YORK (AP) — Trump’s campaign has been indicted for inaction after the head of Northern California pulled his gun at a staff member while driving a jeep in February.
Vincent Bodini, a former software trainer for the state’s campaign, described the incident Wednesday in a state court in Charlotte.
Bodini said before he hoped the matter could be resolved internally, but after waiting for months he realized “it couldn’t happen.”
According to Bodini's description, the head named Earl Phillips "plucked out a pistol, placed his right index finger on the board, and pointed the muzzle at Vincent's kneel."
After complaining to the leadership of the local and regional campaigns, Bodini learned from the dossier that at least four others were going through the same thing.
Bodini said in the complaint: "No shooting does not mean that there will be no harm."
Just two days before the case, the Republican presidential nominee in North Carolina told the masses that if his opponent, Hillary Clinton, was elected and picked her favorite judge, then “people in support of the Second Amendment,” that gun owners and gun enthusiasts, might take action, sparked outrage.
Trump's campaign said the media took his words out of context and he did not advocate violence.
In the complaint, Bodini asked the campaign team to pay compensation for its neglect of supervision, while also asking Philip to pay compensation for his attacks, beatings and mental injuries.
Trump’s spokesman, Hope Hicks, did not immediately respond to the news.
I couldn’t get in touch with Philip.
According to a local Charlotte news agency, WCCB reported that Philippe recently left the campaign team.
His consulting firm, Innovative Consulting Services, did not immediately respond to calls.
Republican presidential candidate Donald Trump’s campaign is controversial.
In this case, the two parties are Bodini and the President of the United States Campaign Company, 16-CS-14300, Mecklenburg (Charlotte) Superior Court.
The UK must pass on the Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow Airport.
Heathrow will also bring vitality and prosperity to our community, London and the country as a whole.
For me, that’s Heathrow’s charm, just as at the 2012 London Olympics, we did everything we could to ensure that our countries and regions were able to benefit from these events.
The Olympics benefited Stratford and the East.
And we’re going to benefit the region once again by creating opportunities for local people and businesses near Heathrow.”
For our local communities alone, we’re going to create 10,000 new apprenticeship jobs, 40,000 new jobs, and free young people around the airport from unemployment.
Just like before the 2012 London Olympics, some people thought Heathrow’s expansion was too large.
“I’ve been told that the UK won’t be able to get ready for the Olympics and we’re going to be humiliated in
Doubts are wrong about the 2012 Olympics, and their skepticism about the Heathrow project will also be proven wrong.
Although there will always be obstacles that need to be overcome, Heathrow is solving every problem.
Like the Olympics, Heathrow works on infrastructure projects for the majority rather than for the few.
The expansion will create as many as 180,000 new jobs and generate £211 billion in economic growth, leaving a fortune in technology and jobs around airports and across the UK.
We have an outstanding Heathrow project team. The team has completed £11 billion of infrastructure investment projects on time and within the budget, including Terminal T5, which has been elected as the world's best airport terminal for five consecutive years, and T2 between two of the world's busiest runways.
Now, they are preparing to build a third runway with £16 billion in private investment.
The United Kingdom has successfully hosted the London 2012 Olympic Games, and I am confident that Heathrow's expansion project will also achieve great success."
It’s a good time for us to pass on the Olympic spirit and expand Heathrow, so we’ll be able to escort our future and create a wealth of technology and jobs that make the UK proud.”
Lord Paul Dayton is chairman of Heathrow’s board of directors and former Commercial Counsel of the United Kingdom’s Treasury.
A woman commits suicide on a Swiss motorway and is killed.
The victims were lying on the highway at midnight.
The woman, who tried to commit suicide, was tossed by multiple vehicles on the highway early on Thursday morning. Police are currently looking for witnesses.
The accident happened on the A1 motorway near Morge, Vau, in the early hours of Thursday.
Vaud police said a woman was "desperately" lying on the road in the middle of the night and had been slapped by multiple cars.
The victim, a 49-year-old Swiss woman, apparently had suicidal intentions. She had been slapped by a number of vehicles, including a van.
The woman died instantly.
According to the police, several of the drivers involved have been investigated, but some drivers have not yet been confirmed. Police are calling on them and witnesses to the accident to come for investigation.
Guillers Hofstetter told the Geneva Tribune that criminal investigations had already been initiated, which was a routine process for such incidents.
At present, it is not known whether the driver will face charges.
Is it possible to avoid collisions?
Hofstetel said: "This will be determined by prosecutors."
Depending on the outcome of two other similar cases over the past five years, drivers will not take responsibility.
According to the newspaper, both cases were caused by someone walking up the highway in the middle of the night. The final verdict was that it was not possible for the driver to expect that pedestrians would cross the highway at midnight.
Last year, a police officer was charged with negligent death in August 2014, when she struck a man and killed him on the A1 highway near Payenne.
The court learned that the 22-year-old victim crossed the road after drinking.
After the investigation, the judge said that the female police officer's driving behavior was completely legal, and that the man's appearance on the road was "completely unexpected and unpredictable", and that the female police officer later dialed an emergency call.
Anyone who can provide information about the incident near Morges on Thursday should contact the police at 021 644 83 46 or 021 644 444.
NASA's climate model: Venus was once suitable for human habitation
According to NASA's new climate model, Venus was once suitable for human habitation.
Scientists at NASA’s Goddard Space Research Institute have developed a model that explores Venus’ past, using tools and algorithms very similar to those used by climate scientists.
The model is used to determine whether Venus, with high surface temperatures, no water, and carbon dioxide in its atmosphere, has ever had an environment similar to Earth’s.
Previously, scientists believed that Venus' rotation rate was slower because its atmosphere was thicker.
However, the latest simulations suggest that Venus may have once had a thinner, Earth-like atmosphere at a slower rate of rotation at the time.
The model also shows the early topography of Venus.
There may be more driest areas of Venus than on Earth, but there used to be plenty of water and geographic changes to harbor life and prevent rapid evaporation.
In the early days, the sunlight shining on Venus was 30 percent fainter than Earth’s.
Anthony del Genio, a scientist at Goddard's Space Research Institute, explained at a press conference: "In the model simulation of the Goddard Space Research Institute, Venus's slow rotation takes nearly two months to make its side."
This warms the surface and produces rainwater that can form thick clouds, which, like an umbrella, block most of the sun’s heat to the ground.
As a result of the simulation, the average climate temperature of Venus is actually several degrees lower than that of Earth today.
The model simulates the evolution of Venus similar to those previously speculated by planetary scientists.
Although there may have been life on Venus, in the course of billions of years, Venus has been exposed to excessive temperatures and ultraviolet rays.
The oceans on the planet have evaporated, and water vapour has also been burned by ultraviolet rays.
With the escape of hydrogen, only the thicker atmosphere of carbon dioxide and deadly greenhouse gas effects are left, resulting in the formation of Venus as we know it today.
The researchers published simulations of a new Venus climate model in the journal Geophysical Research Newsletter.
Patrick Roy resigns as Avalanche manager
Patrick Roy, who stepped down as head coach and vice president of the Colorado Avalanche Ice Hockey team on Thursday, said he had no say in the team’s decision-making level.
Colorado Executive Vice President Joe Sachis confirmed the news and said the club will immediately start looking for a new manager.
In a statement, the 50-year-old said: “For the past three years, it has been my vibrancy, passion and determination to fulfill my Colorado Avalanche head coach and vice president.”
I’ve been thinking hard all summer about how I can improve this team and bring it to the next level.
To do this, the goals of the manager and vice president must be consistent with the goals of the club.
He must also have a voice in decisions that affect the team’s performance.
At present, however, these conditions have not yet been met.
Sages, who hired Roy on May 23, 2013, thanked Roy for his contributions to the team.
"Patrick told me about his decision today," Sages said.
“We are grateful for his contribution to the club and look forward to his success in the future.”
Roy was nominated for the Jack Adams Award for Best Coach in his first season with the Avalanche in 2013-14.
He helped the club get 112 points, ranking first in the Central Division. But Colorado was eliminated by Minnesota in the first round of the playoffs, missing from the playoffs in the last two years.
In the 2015-16 season, the Avalanche team's score was 39 wins and 39 draws and 4 losses, and finally lost the second wild card qualification in the Western League zone with a difference of 5 points.
As a Hall of Fame keeper, Roy has won four Stanley Cups, two each for the Montreal Canadiens and Avalanches.
Workers in the North Sea oil field stop the strike and continue negotiations
Leading negotiators said workers in the North Sea oil fields had halted strikes on pay, and the parties to the dispute had begun a new round of negotiations.
There have been several strikes against oilfield service Wood Group on the North Sea oil platform operated by Royal Dutch Shell.
According to a joint statement, “Wood Group, together with the Unite and RMT unions, can confirm that we have agreed to restart the process of resolving the current dispute.”
“We’re going to start early next week, and we’re going to have multiple full-day meetings in the coming weeks to revisit these issues.”
The union said its recent action was aimed at protesting a pay cut that could reach up to 30 percent, the first in 25 years.
This year, Wood Group has reduced returns for North Sea contractors by about 9 percent due to market pressures.
Prior to the pay cut, the company reduced payments for offshore contractors by 10 percent in 2014.
The company said that another salary cut will help the company through the difficulties of the market depression.
Shell has laid off thousands of employees and has announced plans to shut down oil and gas equipment in 10 countries as a result of the company’s need to streamline its operations following its merger with British energy company British Gas Group.
Energy companies are under pressure from falling crude oil prices as well as Britain’s June Brexit decision.
Dave Anderson, a member of Scotland’s parliament, met with union leaders earlier this week to discuss the North Sea generation’s biggest labour dispute.
He said that the North Sea is crucial for the regional economy.
In a statement, Dave said: “In the North Sea and across the country, we need an economy that serves the majority, not the minority.”
Spending on new projects in the North Sea is not expected to exceed $1.4 billion in 2016, compared with an average of about $7 billion over the past five years.
Kepler's measurement of the rotation velocity of the stars of the Aung Sue cluster
The Kepler space telescope’s latest mission has helped astronomers collect the most complete rotation velocity of the stars.
The new data is helping astronomers understand why the cluster’s stars have different velocities of rotation and the association of rotation periods with the planet’s composition.
Lusa Rebul, a research scientist at the Center for Infrared Processing and Analysis at the California Institute of Technology in Pasadena, said: "We hope that by comparing our results with other clusters, we want to further understand the relationship between stellar mass, age, and even the history of its solar system."
It is 445 light-years from Earth.
Many of the stars in this cluster were born about 125 million years ago, and they are still just reaching adulthood.
They achieve the highest speed of rotation in a lifetime.
When reaching the maximum rotation velocity, these mature stars eject a large number of stellar winds.
When these stellar winds pass through the star's magnetic field, they brake on the stars.
As stars age, their rotation rate begins to slow down.
Kepler measured the rotation velocity of each star by tracking the timing of the appearance of recognizable stellar sunspots (such as sunspots).
The most recent velocity datasets show that larger stars are moving slower than stars with smaller masses.
Reeble compares stars to dancers, and larger dancers spin more slowly than smaller dancers.
“In the ‘ballet show’ of the Aung Sue cluster, we found that stars with slower rotations tend to have larger masses, and the stars with the fastest rotations tend to be the lightest.”
Due to the proximity of the cluster, it can be used as an ideal cosmic laboratory for comparing the mass and rotation velocity of stars.
According to Reeble, “The Aung Sue cluster lays the foundation for a two-way rotating theoretical model of younger and older stars.”
We still have a lot to know about how, when, and why stars slow down their rotation and “put up their own dancing shoes.”
In three new papers, Reeble and her colleagues detailed their latest analysis of the rotation velocity of the star cluster. These three papers will soon be published in the Journal of Astronomy.
Zheng Jianjun, head of the water management section of Nanhu Park, said that the deepest depth of the water bottom of South Lake is more than 10 meters, and those who do not understand the water are likely to be in danger.
"Although the South Lake rescue team is very professional, it is still hoped that the majority of rowing tourists can put on life jackets, do not do illegal actions on the lake, and play safely," Zheng Jianjun said.
The village has a unique marine environment, with natural ecology and cultural ecology two major leisure tourism resources.
